+# Reading consistency implementation
+
+Spec: `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-11-mreader-rc485-ownership-consolidation-design.md`, sections 4 and 11.
+
+This continues the approved implementation. The existing revision-only draft is work in progress, not a published protocol. The source checkout is isolated on `work/rc485-recovery-root`. No live service, database, deployment, or uploaded baseline archive is modified. Runtime qualification remains blocked where dependencies are unavailable.
+
+## Shared protocol (binding for all tasks)
+
+- GET `/api/progress/{series}/{chapter}` returns the **current series resume**; consumers MUST match `chapter_id` before restoring its page. An empty series has revision and session generation zero. All routes derive the account from authentication.
+- POST `/open` accepts `{command_id: UUID, expected_revision: integer >= 0}`. Persist this intent before sending. Retries reuse its exact ID and revision. A successful open assigns a positive server `session_generation`, resets `command_sequence` to zero, advances `revision`, and selects the chapter without borrowing another chapter's checkpoint. A duplicate accepted open does not update recency. `/open` and `/commit` require `X-MReader-Account-ID` from the queued account scope and compare it with the authenticated user before target lookup; missing or different values return HTTP 403 `account_mismatch`. The header is a freshness fence, not identity. Progress reads enforce the comparison only when the header is supplied.
+- POST `/commit` accepts `{command_id: UUID, session_generation: positive integer, command_sequence: positive integer, last_page: integer >= 1, scroll_position: number in [0,1], completed: boolean, completed_page: integer}`. `completed_page` is zero unless explicit end evidence has been observed, then the published page count. This keeps completion evidence independent of the current resume page when the reader scrolls back. Allocate a fresh UUID for every new command and persist the exact transmitted payload; timeout retries reuse it without mutation. Sequence numbers increase within an accepted session.
+- Both commands return a flat canonical reading value with `series_id`, `chapter_id`, `last_page`, `scroll_position`, `updated_at`, `last_opened_at`, `revision`, `session_generation`, `command_sequence`, plus `accepted`, `duplicate`, `code`, and echoed `command_id`. `code` is `accepted`, `duplicate`, `revision_conflict`, `stale_session`, `stale_sequence`, or `command_conflict`. Conflicts return HTTP 200 with `accepted:false`, so clients retain and visibly pause the pending intent; no automatic new open/rebase. Validation is HTTP 400/422; missing/deleted targets 404; temporary storage errors 503; auth 401/403.
+- A duplicate of the latest retained checkpoint identity/payload or retained per-chapter open identity is a no-op. The same retained sequence/ID with a different submitted payload conflicts, even if page clamping would produce the same stored checkpoint. The server keeps no permanent command log: an older exact retry is still fenced by revision/session/sequence, but a superseded ID reused with a new sequence/revision is outside its historical-uniqueness guarantee. Old sessions cannot replace resume or last-open. Exact completion from a recognized older chapter session may merge monotonically, but the response remains a conflict for its resume intent.
+- IDs and server integers are bounded to JavaScript-safe values. Device timestamps are never server ordering keys. No normal runtime Redis progress flusher or progress cache authority.
+- Social consumes a normal `reading_state_v1` view. Library adds `contract_version:1`, `generated_at`, `request_identity:{scope,state,sort,offset,limit}`, `recently_opened:{items,total}`, item `reading_revision`, `reading_available`, and `reading_action:{kind,chapter_id,chapter_slug}` or null. Recent items are global history, at most 12, from the same query as summary and main items. Existing fields remain.
+
+### Task 1: Web shared pending repository
+
+Implement only `frontend/**` and a focused Node-runnable test under `tests/regression/`. Read the shared protocol above and spec section 4. Existing dirty Web changes belong to this task and may be replaced. Do not edit Go, SQL, Android, Social, or API Python tests. Do not spawn subagents.
+
+1. Write failing behavioral tests for generation-N ACK racing generation N+1, retry payload stability, stale-session/conflict retention, scope/account switch, separate completion evidence, and pending-only overlays. Exercise production reducer/repository code using Node's TypeScript stripping where possible; do not substitute source-string assertions.
+2. Replace Reader's writable localStorage checkpoint path with one asynchronous IndexedDB-backed reading repository. Keep immediate memory state, transactional conditional ACK handling, account+origin isolation, persisted open/commit intents, and cross-tab notifications. Coalesce local writes to one second and dirty network flushes to 30 seconds plus end/navigation/foreground/online. One in-flight series/session; bounded exponential jitter retry up to 60 seconds. Handle storage failure visibly, cap 500 pending chapter/session records and 5 MiB/account without discarding dirty intent, prune confirmed entries after seven days. No guest-to-account upload.
+3. Wire Reader open, restore, checkpoint, explicit completion, account change, and sync status to the repository. Preserve protected delivery. Completion requires an observed end and successfully loaded relevant media, not a placeholder/manifest alone. Preserve completion evidence while resuming an earlier page. Cancel stale restore/navigation callbacks. A rejected command remains visible and paused until explicit reconciliation; do not silently rebase. Beacon acceptance never acknowledges a command.
+4. Make affected Library/Series/Browse surfaces subscribe to pending/confirmed reading repository updates, invalidate server reads after ACK, and use one canonical Library response for counts and recent rail. Pending previews do not rewrite global counts, furthest, or completion. Add the declared response fields to the adapter and use the server's recent list, not an independent History fetch.
+5. Warn before explicit logout if private unsynced intent would be lost; clear it after authorized logout and prevent stale in-flight responses from restoring it. Keep authoritative auth-failure behavior.
+6. Run the focused real tests. Run Web typecheck/build if dependencies exist; otherwise record exact blockers, do not mark them passed. Self-review race handling and keep changes limited to this task. Commit ONLY assigned files after checks; provide a report with tests and limitations.
+
+### Task 2: Android shared pending repository
+
+Implement only `android/**` and focused Android tests. Read the shared protocol above and spec section 4. Existing dirty Android changes belong to this task and may be replaced. Do not edit other domains. Do not spawn subagents.
+
+1. Consolidate local Progress and separate recent-read overlays behind the existing reading repository. Preserve immediate UI, account+origin scope, asynchronous durable writes, monotonic local generation, stable open/commit payloads, accepted session/sequence, explicit completion evidence, and conditional ACK cleanup.
+2. Implement the same retry, pending bounds, conflict pause, logout/privacy, dirty-only interval, and end/media semantics as Task 1 and the spec. Remove obsolete invisible endpoint preferences once and consistently use the configured origin. SharedPreferences disk failure must not be reported as durable success.
+3. Wire the API adapter/models and Reader/Library/Browse/Series projections to the shared protocol and single Library recent rail. Restore a server checkpoint only for its actual chapter. Retire competing timestamp/rank overlays and the sleep awaiting a Redis flusher.
+4. Add focused behavioral Kotlin tests for ACK races, session/sequence payloads, scope switch and conflict preservation. Run Gradle compilation/tests if available and record blockers honestly. Self-review and commit ONLY assigned files.
+
+### Task 3: Integrated review and qualification
+
+Review backend ordering, migration/view, both clients, and Library together. Verify matched methods/payloads, concurrent devices/tabs, duplicate/timeout retries, no false completion, monotonic revision, exact ledger markers, and read-after-write on actual PostgreSQL. Inspect original capability preservation. Record each real command/output and every blocked gate separately; no release ZIP until the combined approved scope is qualified.
